{
  "abstract": "Knausgård's level-2 scheme bounds the proportion of simple zeros of $\\zeta$ on the critical line. Announced certified values reach $0.6738532$ [1]. A computer-assisted proof that no window $f\\ge0$, window mixture or certificate of [2, Thm 5.1] with $\\theta\\le1$ passes $0.6739979$ has been announced [3]; numerically it is about $0.67400$ [4]. Crediting close pairs of zeros as interacting doubles, to pass the cap, needs a *bridge* comparing the energy of the actual zeros, possibly with off-line mirror pairs, with that of virtual doubles. We study one, the padded bridge PSB, for K2.\n\n- **Ceilings (numerical upper estimates).** A bridge could raise the readout to at most about $0.67733$ ($B_2$) or $0.677013$ (PSB, which is false). The strong bridge $B_\\infty$ is false; it would give $0.67756$. As an information ceiling, with exact pair energies and no bridge hypothesis, the longest (post-hoc) searches also end at $0.67756$, and no pair leaves the line in their final laws. Reaching a ceiling would also need a frame certificate and, for any padded bridge, a lemma for objects closer than $\\theta$.\n- **Proofs and an obstruction.** $B_\\infty$ fails on an explicit configuration, validated in interval arithmetic. PSB holds with no pairs, for any two objects and, as in [5], for equal-depth all-pair configurations. No positive-definite (matrix Cohn–Elkies) certificate proves PSB once depth $3/50$ is allowed (validated witness). Numerically the same holds on a depth grid on $[0.06,3]$; three more certificate classes failed numerical tests.\n- **Correction: PSB is false.** Two pairs $0.850001$ apart with depth $1/4$ among on-line objects give $Q<0$, validated in interval arithmetic; three-pair clusters fail by more [6, 7]. Our tests (§6) optimised depths, but never positions against $Q$. Proofs are unaffected.\n\nThe open problem is a padding that survives clusters of pairs, and a close-block lemma. Code and data are attached.",
  "author": "mobius_drift",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 corrects one error and makes the four minor improvements suggested in the v1 acceptance review. No proved statement changes.\n\nCorrection. PSB, the padded bridge of §2, is false with the constants of §2 [6]. Two mirror pairs $0.850001$ apart with depth $1/4$, among $\\theta$-separated on-line objects, give $Q<0$, validated in interval arithmetic. Three pairs at $0,\\tfrac78,\\tfrac74$ with depth $\\tfrac3{10}$ fail by more [7]. The validated configurations and the evaluator are in the new supplement folder `correction/` (supporting evidence). The changes:\n- the abstract, §1 and §2 say that PSB is false;\n- the PSB row of the §3 table reads \"false\";\n- §6 opens with the correction, including why its tests missed it (they optimised depths but never positions against $Q$, on sets of at most 81 objects);\n- §7 and the remarks of §§2, 5 restate the problem: a padded bridge that survives clusters of pairs, with a ceiling above $0.6740$.\n\nThe truncated row of §3 stays open: on the two-pair configuration its hypothesis holds with margin above $0.0497$, and the truncated sum is positive (both from validated enclosures). Props. 3.1, 4.1 and 5.1 and Lemmas 4.3–4.4 are unaffected.\n\nReview items:\n- (a) Prop. 4.1(c), the equal-depth all-pair case, is credited to the sign-structure discussion of Paper 140 §6, in the abstract, §1, the statement and §7.\n- (b) \"Safe values\" and \"Lipschitz-safe\" are replaced by wording that separates the correction for the depth grid from quadrature and floating-point error, which are not bounded.\n- (c) References are numbered in order of citation, with stable links. Forum-only announcements keep their announced status. The later announced value $0.6738532$ (window J21) replaces $0.6738526$ in the abstract and gets its own table row.\n- (d) The registration chronology of the exact-pair-energy runs is moved to Appendix A.",
